n at chevron voted  to force that company  to cut its carbon emissions pressure to  change is coming from both outside and  inside big oil there are people that  really want to see this fundamental  change  and it's you know getting right into the  boardroom and that means that  we're going to start to see these  companies responding and making some of  these tough decisions  in canada there are growing challenges  attracting investments in the western  oil patch  one academic says companies more than  governments see the need to become more  nimble it's no surprise to them  but it's a surprise to our political  leaders in alberta and saskatchewan  they have placed all their bets  on the fossil fuel industry but  governments too could see their hands  forced  the court is satisfied that a duty of  care should be recognized  an australian court judgment from a  lawsuit brought by a group of teenagers  ruled the government has a duty to  protect future generations from climate  change  in this case when it comes to expanding  coal production this case was about  young people stepping up and demanding  more from the adults whose actions are  determining our future well-being  more evidence friday that big oil  companies get it  french oil and gas giant total announced  a rebranded image  the future will see total become a  multi-energy enterprise what the ceo  calls  a green energy company in the majority  grassroots climate advocates say genuine  change will come  not just when oil companies diversify  but when they stop  investing in new oil and gas projects  eric sorensen global news  toronto the oil and gas sector is at a  crossroads still ahead  the path to decarbonization here  as countries work to decarbonize canada  has been slowed  by an economy deeply grounded in oil and  gas  yet this country is quietly becoming a  leader in technology  they could be indispensable in bridging  that gap  i spoke with derek evans he's the  president and ceo of meg energy  a leading oil company that's setting an  ambitious goal of net zero  based on the prospect of carbon  the oil and gas sector is at a  crossroads and for derrick evans the way  out  is through decarbonization so  to me it's a natural step in the  evolution of the oil and gas business  like canada itself meg energy aims to  achieve net zero emissions by 2050.  for evans the only realistic path  includes carbon capture  it's the single biggest lever that we  have to pull to  actually start to decarbonize this is a  technology that exists today  this is one that we could put in the  field in one sense carbon capture is the  most direct solution to the emissions  problem  simply return the carbon to the earth as  fossil fuels are burned instead of being  released into the air the co2 is  chemically isolated and captured  then compressed and sent via pipeline to  a location to be stored underground  globally there are more than two dozen  such facilities  but canada is home to some of the  largest and most advanced  including the boundary dam in  saskatchewan the quest facility near  edmonton  and the alberta carbon trunk line and  it's near christina lake alberta  where meg energy hopes to erase most of  its own emissions  the biggest barrier for evidence the  endless political fighting over the  carbon tax  a clear price on admissions that  incentivizes future planning  for anybody to undertake a carbon  capture and storage project  they're going to want to know that there  is going to be a  contractually set price of carbon that  they can take to the bank  and say look we want to build this  project that sort of uncertainty  has been a big big problem for  people in the business that wanted to  move forward on carbon capture and  storage  and while critics point to its high cost  in long time lines  the federal budget introduced a  corporate tax credit for carbon capture  it's just a fraction of the 30 billion  dollars in funding  that alberta is asking ottawa for in the  